# rvv_gemm_tutorial.md - GEMM tutorial notebook + HW-config sync

Goal: a second end-user notebook (docs/andes_sim_gemm_tutorial.ipynb) that
walks an 8x8x8 RVV-intrinsic GEMM through the product, plus the two driver
features the story needs: engine HW parameters visible in `list engines`,
and the QEMU fast leg inheriting the selected engine's HW config.

## Features

### F1: hw-sync (behavioral, driver)
The hybrid fast leg's `-cpu andes-ax45mpv,vlen=...` is today hardcoded to
512. Make it follow the selected engine: the fast-leg invoke template gains
a `{vlen}` placeholder; `plan_argvs` fills it from the accurate-leg
engine's descriptor (fallback 512 when the engine reports none, e.g. a
scalar core). ISA sync: an engine with xlen != 64 (d23 is rv32) is
rejected by `run --model hybrid` with a clean error - the fast leg is
rv64-only. The hybrid banner prints the synced `vlen=` so users see it.
- Key files: driver/engine_registry.cpp, driver/run_plan.cpp,
  driver/cmd_run.cpp, driver/plan_test.cpp (RED first).
- State: done

### F2: hw-params (behavioral, driver + vsim submodule)
`sim_<cfg> --describe` additionally reports `dlen` and `elen` (scanned
from NDS_DLEN / NDS_ELEN in the embedded config.inc; 0 = absent).
The driver parses them and `list engines` grows XLEN/DLEN/ELEN columns
next to VLEN ("-" when an engine does not report a value).
- Key files: verilator/src/main.cpp (submodule commit + pointer bump),
  driver/engine_descriptor.hpp, driver/cmd_list.cpp,
  driver/json_test.cpp (RED first).
- Depends on: nothing (driver side testable with JSON fixtures; stale
  engine binaries simply show "-" until rebuilt).
- State: done (all five sim_* engines rebuilt with the new describe)


### F3: gemm-demo-program
gemm.c (repo-root scratch, embedded in the notebook): plain-C init of
A/B, 8x8x8 int32 GEMM kernel in RVV intrinsics (row-accumulate:
vle32/vmacc.vx/vse32, e32m1, vl=8), scalar-C reference + verify printing
PASS, ROI markers around the kernel call. Two-flag vplat build with
-march=rv64gcv. Verified on fast (PASS) and hybrid marker (5068 cycles).
- State: done

### F4: hotloop-pc-workflow
QEMU/scripts/hotloops.sh (LLM report mode) on the markerless gemm ELF
finds the hottest RVV loop = the gemm kernel; objdump gives the kernel
entry/ret addresses; `run --model hybrid --trigger pc` measures between
them; figure compared against the marker run (5002 vs 5068 cycles).
- Bugs found and fixed on the way (failing test first for each):
  - QEMU/scripts/hotloops.sh: objdump -t visibility tokens (".hidden")
    shifted the size field and killed the symbol parse with a gawk fatal
    (tests/plugin/test_plugin_hotloops_hidden_syms.sh).
  - qemu_plugin/hybrid_handoff.c: pc/icount/slice drains reported
    vaddr+size as the resume PC while capturing PRE-execution state, so
    the boundary insn executed on neither leg (a skipped vsetivli left
    vtype.vill set and faulted the vsim window). Boundary drains now
    resume AT the boundary insn; markers keep the NOP-skip semantics
    (tests/plugin/test_plugin_pc_trigger_resume_pc.sh).
- Follow-up: hotloops.sh LLM report now emits a ready window per loop,
  `measure: --pc-start 0x<entry> --pc-end 0x<ret>` of the enclosing
  function (the loop's own pc_range spans one iteration under one-shot
  pc triggers); the notebook lifts the flags from the report and the
  objdump step is gone (tests/plugin/test_plugin_hotloops_measure_window.sh).
- State: done

